ML_PAGE("l12-prep", "<header class=\"page-header theme-l12\"><span class=\"eyebrow\">CHAPTER 12 · MCQ PREPARATION</span><h2>Chapter 12 MCQ Prep</h2><p>The complete MCQ preparation notes in their original order: 30 multiple-choice questions sit inside the sections they belong to, with the answer, the logic and the exam trap hidden until you reveal them.</p></header><div class=\"study-grid three-col\"><div class=\"metric-card\"><strong>30</strong><span>MCQs in the notes</span></div><div class=\"metric-card\"><strong>30</strong><span>Exam traps explained</span></div><div class=\"metric-card\"><strong>30</strong><span>Difficult-mode questions</span></div></div><div class=\"action-row\"><button class=\"primary-btn bg-l12\" data-quiz=\"12\">Start Chapter 12 simulator</button></div><details class=\"topic-index toc-panel theme-l12\" id=\"l12p-toc\"><summary><strong>MCQ notes</strong><span class=\"toc-count\">2 sections</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><input type=\"search\" class=\"toc-search\" placeholder=\"Filter sections...\" aria-label=\"Filter sections\"><ul class=\"toc-list\"><li class=\"toc-group\">PART A - PRACTICE QUESTIONS</li><li data-full=\"Questions\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l12p-s2\" title=\"Questions\">Practice questions</button></li><li class=\"toc-group\">PART B - FORMULAS AND ERROR DIAGNOSIS</li><li data-full=\"Formula map and error diagnosis\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l12p-s4\" title=\"Formula map and error diagnosis\">Formula map and error diagnosis</button></li></ul></details><details class=\"prep-notes theme-l12\" open><summary><div><strong>Chapter 12 MCQ preparation</strong><small>Exam strategy, practice questions, formula sheets and review - section by section, answers hidden.</small></div><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"markdown-body compact\"><div class=\"notes-toolbar\"><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"open\">Expand all</button><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"close\">Collapse all</button><span>2 sections · click a heading to open it</span></div><h2>Chapter 12 - Mutual Funds and ETFs: MCQ Practice</h2>\n<p><strong>30 questions · suggested time: 45 minutes · practice target: 80%.</strong> These are illustrative study suggestions, not official exam requirements. Choose the single best answer before checking the separate answer key. Examples use the stated fees, timing and assumptions; taxes are ignored unless specified.</p>\n<p>Use the sequence <strong>investor objective → fund holdings and structure → risks and diversification → all costs → relevant benchmark and outcome</strong>. A fund is a vehicle: its investment strategy and underlying assets determine much of its risk.</p><h2 class=\"notes-divider\" id=\"l12p-d1\">PART A - PRACTICE QUESTIONS</h2><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l12p-s2\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Questions</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q1</span><div><p>A mutual fund pools money from investors to buy securities. What does an investor normally own?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> A separate direct title to each underlying share in the same quantity as the fund.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Shares or units in the fund, representing an interest in its portfolio.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> A guaranteed fixed return from every security in the portfolio.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> An obligation to manage the fund's trades personally.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>The shareholder owns an interest in the fund, which owns its underlying assets.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Pooled exposure is not the same as direct ownership of every underlying share.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q2</span><div><p>Why can a diversified fund reduce some investment risks?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> It guarantees a positive return each year.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Diversification eliminates all market risk.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Losses specific to one holding may be partly offset by other holdings, though broad market risk remains.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Every fund is equally diversified regardless of its holdings.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Diversification can reduce security-specific exposure, but broad or concentrated risks remain.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> More holdings alone do not guarantee safety.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q3</span><div><p>An open-end mutual fund has RM105 million in assets, RM5 million in liabilities and 10 million shares. What is its NAV per share?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> RM11.00.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> RM10.50.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> RM10.00.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> RM100.00.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>NAV per share = (RM105m - RM5m) / 10m = <strong>RM10.00</strong>.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Subtract liabilities before dividing; the source slide's simplified expression omits them.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q4</span><div><p>What is the usual transaction mechanism for a traditional open-end mutual fund?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The fund can never issue or redeem shares.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Every share must trade continuously on a stock exchange at a negotiated price.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Investors transact with the fund at the applicable calculated NAV, subject to its dealing and fee terms.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Investors always receive a guaranteed price above NAV.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Traditional open-end fund dealing uses the applicable NAV and fund terms, with creation/redemption through the fund.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Intraday secondary-market trading is characteristic of ETFs and closed-end shares, not the usual open-end mechanism.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q5</span><div><p>A closed-end fund's NAV is RM20 per share while its exchange price is RM18. What is the price relative to NAV?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> A 20% premium.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> A 10% premium.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> A 2% discount.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> A 10% discount.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Discount = (20 - 18) / 20 = <strong>10% of NAV</strong>.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Divide the difference by NAV, not the market price, under this convention.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q6</span><div><p>A closed-end fund trades at RM21 while its NAV is RM20. Its premium is:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 20%.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 1%.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 10%.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 5%.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Premium = (21 - 20) / 20 = <strong>5% of NAV</strong>.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A premium is an observation, not an automatic guarantee of overvaluation.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q7</span><div><p>Which description best fits a typical ETF?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Its market price must equal NAV at every instant.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Its shares trade on an exchange; an authorized participant creation/redemption mechanism can help keep price close to NAV but does not guarantee equality.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Every ETF is actively managed.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> It cannot hold bonds or cash.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>ETFs trade on exchanges and creation/redemption by authorized participants can support price-NAV alignment, though deviations can occur.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> An arbitrage mechanism does not guarantee exact equality at all times.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q8</span><div><p>Which statement correctly distinguishes a vehicle from a strategy?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> An ETF can never be actively managed.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> All ETFs are index funds, and all index funds are ETFs.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> An index-tracking strategy can be offered through either an ETF or an open-end mutual fund.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> An index fund must guarantee performance above its benchmark.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>ETF is a trading/vehicle structure; index tracking is a strategy that can be used in several fund structures.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Vehicle and investment strategy are different dimensions.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q9</span><div><p>A front-end load is 5% of an RM1,000 gross investment. If the remaining amount is invested at NAV, how much enters the fund?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> RM500.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> RM1,000.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> RM1,050.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> RM950.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Load = RM1,000 x 5% = RM50; invested amount = <strong>RM950</strong>.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> The question specifies the load as a percentage of the gross contribution.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q10</span><div><p>An investor receives RM9,800 when redeeming a fund investment worth RM10,000 before a back-end load. What is the load under these stated figures?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> RM20, or 0.2%.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> RM200, or 2% of the pre-fee redemption value.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> RM500, or 5%.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> There is no fee because the investor is selling.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Load = RM10,000 - RM9,800 = RM200, or <strong>2%</strong> of RM10,000.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A back-end charge is deducted on redemption under its stated terms.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q11</span><div><p>A no-load fund is best described as:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> A fund without a sales load under its stated terms; other ongoing expenses may still apply.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> A fund that can never charge any operating fee.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> A fund that guarantees the market return.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> A fund with no investment risk.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>No sales load does not rule out management or other ongoing costs.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> No-load is not the same as no-fee.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q12</span><div><p>A fund with average net assets of RM100 million charges a 1% annual management fee on those average assets. What is the approximate annual management fee?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> RM1 million.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> RM100,000.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> RM10 million.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> RM99 million.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>RM100m x 1% = <strong>RM1m</strong> annually, using average net assets and a constant rate.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> The question concerns a fund-level charge, not 1% of the fund's gross returns.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q13</span><div><p>Which distinction about ongoing fund fees is most accurate?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> A management fee compensates investment management; a U.S. 12b-1 fee generally relates to distribution and/or shareholder-service expenses.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> A 12b-1 fee is identical to the purchase price of all securities in the portfolio.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> A no-load fund cannot have any management fee.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Fees have no possible effect on an investor's net return.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Management and 12b-1 expenses serve distinct functions; the latter generally supports distribution and/or shareholder services.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> The slide's description of 12b-1 as a general management fee is oversimplified.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q14</span><div><p>An investor wants broad exposure to a specified stock index. Which comparison is most useful when selecting between two index funds tracking that same benchmark?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Assume both must have identical net returns because their index names match.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Select the fund with the longest name.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Compare total costs, tracking difference, fund structure and how each fits the investor's account and needs.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Ignore trading costs and tracking because both own securities.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Costs, tracking differences, liquidity and account suitability can create different investor outcomes even for the same benchmark.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A shared index label does not guarantee equal net performance.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q15</span><div><p>A fund holds shares only in a single industry. What risk deserves particular attention?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The fund has no exposure to stock prices.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> All market and industry risks have been diversified away.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Sector concentration: several holdings may be exposed to the same adverse industry event.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> A sector label guarantees superior returns.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Securities in one sector can fall together when that sector experiences a shock.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Many holdings do not automatically imply broad diversification.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q16</span><div><p>Which fund most directly aims to combine income and capital appreciation through holdings in both equities and bonds?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> A balanced fund, subject to its actual portfolio and mandate.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> A fund holding only one speculative stock.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> A fund that holds exclusively one issuer's short-term debt.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> A fund whose objective explicitly excludes both income and growth.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>A balanced fund typically combines equities and bonds, though allocations and risk differ by fund.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A name alone does not guarantee a particular fixed allocation or safety.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q17</span><div><p>A growth fund and a value fund can best be distinguished by:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Their investment approaches: growth emphasizes growth prospects, while value seeks securities assessed as inexpensive relative to fundamentals.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> A guarantee that the growth fund always earns more.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> A rule that value stocks cannot ever pay dividends.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> A guarantee that neither fund can lose money.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Growth and value describe investment approaches or styles; neither guarantees an outcome.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Different styles can overlap and both carry risk.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q18</span><div><p>A bond fund holds many bonds. Which claim is most accurate?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> It is guaranteed to return a fixed principal on a particular date like a single bond held to maturity.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> It can diversify issuer exposure, but its NAV may fall when yields rise and it still faces portfolio-specific risks.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> It is immune to interest-rate movements.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Every bond fund has identical credit and duration risk.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>A bond fund can hold diversified bonds yet its share value changes with yields, credit and other risks.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A perpetual fund is not equivalent to holding one bond until a specified maturity.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q19</span><div><p>Which statement about a money market fund is most defensible?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Its yield can never change with interest rates.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Every money market fund worldwide always maintains exactly one unit of local currency per share.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> It holds short-term instruments, but its NAV and protections depend on its structure and rules; it is not universally guaranteed risk-free.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> It holds only long-term common stocks.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Money market funds generally hold short-term instruments, but stable NAV and investor protections depend on the fund and jurisdiction.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> The slide's universal <span class=\"cur\">$</span>1 NAV characterization is not generally valid.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q20</span><div><p>An internationally invested fund is held by an investor whose home currency differs from the currencies of its underlying assets. Which risk matters?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Foreign-currency effects never matter inside a pooled fund.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Exchange-rate changes can affect the investor's home-currency return in addition to the assets' local returns.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The fund cannot own more than one security.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> A foreign investment automatically eliminates all market risk.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Exchange-rate changes can raise or lower the home-currency value of foreign assets and distributions.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Pooling does not automatically remove currency exposure.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q21</span><div><p>What is a unit investment trust commonly designed to provide?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Guaranteed active trading by the investor every hour.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> An interest in a generally fixed portfolio for a specified trust term, subject to its actual terms.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The same legal structure as every hedge fund worldwide.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Guaranteed return of principal.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>A UIT typically holds a relatively fixed portfolio for a specified term; consult its actual rules and holdings.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A fixed portfolio does not guarantee principal.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q22</span><div><p>Which distinction between equity and mortgage REITs is most useful?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Equity REITs primarily own or operate properties; mortgage REITs primarily hold real-estate debt or related assets.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> All REITs hold only corporate common shares.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Every REIT is necessarily a closed-end investment company.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> REIT income and prices cannot change.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Equity and mortgage REITs have different primary exposures.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> The chapter's description of every REIT as a closed-end investment company is an overgeneralization.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q23</span><div><p>Which statement about hedge funds is most accurate in this course comparison?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Their structures, strategies, fees and eligible investors vary; they should not be assumed equivalent to ordinary retail mutual funds.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Every hedge fund worldwide has the same guaranteed return.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> All hedge funds are government-insured deposits.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> None can use leverage or short positions.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Hedge-fund structures and requirements vary by jurisdiction and fund; the course's historic eligibility and fee examples are not universal current rules.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> \"Hedge fund\" is not a guarantee of hedging or positive performance.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q24</span><div><p>What does automatic dividend reinvestment generally do?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Guarantees the investor pays no applicable tax.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Uses distributions to acquire additional fund shares under the plan's terms.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Prevents the fund's NAV from falling.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Doubles the investor's wealth immediately.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>A reinvestment plan applies distributions to more fund shares according to its rules.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Reinvestment does not by itself eliminate tax or investment losses.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q25</span><div><p>An investor buys a fund share for RM20, receives RM0.60 income and RM0.40 capital-gain distribution in cash, then sells for RM21. What is holding-period return, ignoring fees and tax?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 5%.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 10%.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 7%.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 15%.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>HPR = $(21-20+0.60+0.40)/20=2/20=\\mathbf{10\\%}$.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Include both types of cash distribution and the price change.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q26</span><div><p>An investor holds 100 fund shares bought at RM10 each. A RM0.50-per-share distribution is fully reinvested when shares cost RM10; the investor ends the period with 105 shares worth RM10.50 each. What is the holding-period return, ignoring fees and tax?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 10.25%.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 5.00%.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 15.25%.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 52.50%.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Ending value = 105 x RM10.50 = RM1,102.50; initial value = RM1,000; return = RM102.50 / RM1,000 = <strong>10.25%</strong>.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Reinvesting increases the share count used in ending value.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q27</span><div><p>Why should the RM50 distribution in Q26 not be added again to the ending value when using the 105 shares?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The share count remains 100 after reinvestment.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Reinvested distributions have no economic value.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Fund distributions always disappear from the calculation.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> It already purchased the five additional shares included in the ending value.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>The RM50 bought five shares, already included in the 105-share ending value.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Adding the distribution again double counts it.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q28</span><div><p>A fund's published return is 8% after its ongoing operating expenses but before a particular investor's transaction costs. What is the correct treatment of the ongoing expense ratio when using that published net return?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Assume all investors experience precisely 8% after every possible cost.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Always subtract the same ongoing fee a second time.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Add the expense ratio to the return.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Do not subtract those already-reflected operating expenses again; account separately for applicable investor-specific costs.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>The given published return already reflects those operating expenses; other investor-specific charges may still apply.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Subtracting the same fee twice understates return.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q29</span><div><p>Which is generally the most suitable benchmark for assessing a diversified domestic large-cap equity index fund?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Last year's best-performing single stock.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> A cash account regardless of the fund's equity exposure.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> A bond index with unrelated duration and credit risks.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Its stated domestic large-cap equity index or another closely matched benchmark.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>An appropriately matched benchmark reflects the fund's investment universe and risk profile.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> An unrelated benchmark can make performance comparisons misleading.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q30</span><div><p>An investor needs accessible capital for a near-term obligation and cannot absorb a substantial loss. A friend recommends putting all of it into a volatile sector fund because last year's return was high. What is the strongest response?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Ignore the fund's holdings because its name contains \"fund.\"</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Buy immediately because last year's winner must repeat.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Sector funds eliminate all losses through pooling.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Assess horizon, liquidity, loss tolerance, concentration and costs; a volatile concentrated stock fund may not fit the stated need.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>A concentrated volatile equity fund can conflict with near-term liquidity and loss constraints; last year's performance is insufficient.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A fund structure does not make an unsuitable strategy safe.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details><h2 class=\"notes-divider\" id=\"l12p-d3\">PART B - FORMULAS AND ERROR DIAGNOSIS</h2><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l12p-s4\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Formula map and error diagnosis</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"l12_distribution\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>A distribution lowers NAV by the amount paid out: total value is unchanged until the market moves.</figcaption></figure></div><ul>\n<li><strong>NAV per share</strong> = (total assets - total liabilities) / shares outstanding.</li>\n<li><strong>Premium or discount to NAV</strong> = (market price - NAV) / NAV; a negative result is a discount.</li>\n<li><strong>Cash-distribution HPR</strong> = (ending price - beginning price + cash distributions) / beginning price, assuming one share and no additional purchases or sales. For reinvestment, calculate ending value using the resulting share count and avoid adding those distributions twice.</li>\n<li>Front-end and back-end loads depend on their stated base. Ongoing expenses may already be reflected in a reported net return; do not subtract the same cost twice.</li>\n<li>Assess holdings, concentration, market and currency exposure, fees, trading characteristics, benchmark, objective and time horizon. Compare the actual prospectus and fee schedule.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Categorize missed questions as structure/strategy confusion, NAV or fee denominator, return double counting, benchmark mismatch, or an unsupported safety claim. Ask which change in assumptions would change your answer.</p>\n<p><em>Source note:</em> Original practice questions based on Chapter 12, <em>Mutual Funds and Exchange-Traded Funds</em> (Pearson slides, 2014). Slide descriptions of NAV, 12b-1 fees, money-market NAV and REIT structure are qualified above. Fee, tax and legal treatment depends on the applicable fund and jurisdiction; practice targets are illustrative.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"question-bank-wrap hard-wrap\" id=\"l12-hard\"><summary><div><strong>Difficult mode - 30 questions</strong><small>18 Bloom-graded questions (3 per level) and 12 marked extension questions. Answers hidden.</small></div><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><p class=\"hard-intro\">Questions climb Bloom&#39;s taxonomy from <b>Remember</b> to <b>Create</b>. The <b>extension</b> questions go beyond the slides (industry practice, CFA-style, case competitions, Master&#39;s, PhD and postdoc level) but can be reasoned out from the chapter&#39;s principles.</p><div class=\"action-row\"><button class=\"primary-btn\" data-quiz=\"12\" data-quiz-mode=\"difficult\">Start Chapter 12 difficult mode</button><button class=\"secondary-btn\" data-quiz=\"12\" data-quiz-mode=\"extensions\">Extensions only</button></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-1\">Remember</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D1</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-1\">Remember</span></div><p>In the organization of a mutual fund, which party <strong>physically safeguards</strong> the fund's securities?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The transfer agent</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The distributor</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The custodian</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The investment adviser</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>The custodian holds and safeguards the assets, keeping them separate from the manager. The transfer agent keeps ownership records and processes purchases and redemptions (A), the distributor sells the fund's shares (B), and the adviser chooses the investments (D).</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D2</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-1\">Remember</span></div><p>In a US mutual fund fee table, which item is a <strong>shareholder fee paid directly by the investor</strong> rather than an annual fund operating expense?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The management fee</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The distribution and service (12b-1) fee</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Other expenses such as administration and custody</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The maximum sales charge (load) on purchases</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Table 12.1 separates shareholder fees (sales loads, deferred loads, exchange and account fees), which come out of the investor's own money, from annual operating expenses (management, 12b-1 and other expenses), which are paid from fund assets and show up in NAV. A, B and C are all operating expenses.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D3</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-1\">Remember</span></div><p>According to the chapter, a mutual fund investor's return can come from which three sources?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Income distributions, capital-gain distributions, and the change in the share's NAV or price</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Management-fee rebates paid to long-term holders, sales-load refunds on redemption, and the change in the fund's NAV</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Dividends received by the fund, the expense ratio, and the portfolio turnover rate</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Interest on uninvested cash, the 12b-1 fee, and the bid-ask spread</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Slide 12-41 lists dividend income, capital-gains distributions and the change in price or NAV (which includes unrealized paper gains). Fees, turnover and spreads (B, C, D) reduce returns; they are not sources of return.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D4</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span></div><p>An open-end fund issues new shares every day. Why does this <strong>not</strong> dilute the existing shareholders?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Because the fund guarantees that NAV will not fall after new money arrives.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Because new shares are issued at NAV, so net assets and the share count rise in the same proportion.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Because each new investor's sales load is paid into the fund and added to net assets, raising NAV for existing holders enough to offset the new shares.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Because the number of shares in an open-end fund is fixed after the initial offering.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>If net assets are $\\text{RM}100$ million on $10$ million shares ($NAV = \\text{RM}10$) and $\\text{RM}10$ million arrives for $1$ million new shares, NAV stays $\\dfrac{110}{11} = \\text{RM}10$. No guarantee is involved (A); a load goes to the distributor, not into NAV (C); a fixed share count describes a closed-end fund (D).</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D5</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span></div><p>Both an ETF and a closed-end fund trade on an exchange. Why does the ETF's price usually stay much closer to NAV?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> ETFs are always index funds, and index funds cannot trade away from NAV because their holdings are published every day, so any gap would be spotted by ordinary investors and traded away within minutes.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Closed-end funds publish NAV only once a year, so investors cannot compare the share price with the value of the underlying portfolio.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Authorized participants can create or redeem ETF shares in exchange for the basket, which rewards closing a gap; a closed-end fund's share count is essentially fixed, so it has no such channel.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Exchange rules forbid an ETF from trading more than $1\\%$ away from its NAV, and trading is halted whenever that band is breached until the price comes back.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>The creation and redemption channel lets an AP profit from a premium (create and sell) or a discount (buy and redeem), which pushes the price toward NAV. A closed-end fund has no comparable mechanism, so its discount can persist. Not every ETF is an index fund (A), closed-end funds report NAV regularly (B), and no rule fixes a $1\\%$ band (D).</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D6</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span></div><p>No bond in a diversified bond fund has defaulted, yet the fund's NAV has fallen $6\\%$ this year. What is the most likely explanation?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The fund must have charged a back-end load to all shareholders.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> A bond fund's NAV can only fall when a holding defaults or is downgraded, so a $6\\%$ fall with no defaults must be an error in the NAV calculation.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The expense ratio is deducted once a year as a $6\\%$ lump sum.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Market yields rose, lowering the market value of its bonds; unlike a single bond, the fund has no maturity date at which par is repaid.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>NAV marks the bonds at market value, and bond prices fall when yields rise, more so for long durations. The fund keeps replacing its bonds, so there is no maturity date that \"pulls\" it back to par. A back-end load affects only redeeming investors (A); default is one risk among several (B); expense ratios are far smaller than $6\\%$ and accrue gradually (C).</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D7</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span></div><p>A fund holds securities worth $\\text{RM}180$ million and cash of $\\text{RM}12$ million. It has accrued expenses and other payables of $\\text{RM}7$ million and borrowings of $\\text{RM}5$ million. There are $16$ million shares outstanding. What is NAV per share?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> $\\text{RM}11.25$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> $\\text{RM}12.00$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> $\\text{RM}11.56$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> $\\text{RM}10.50$</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>$NAV = \\dfrac{180 + 12 - 7 - 5}{16} = \\dfrac{180}{16} = \\mathbf{\\text{RM}11.25}$. B ignores all liabilities; C subtracts only the payables and forgets the borrowings; D treats cash as a liability instead of an asset.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D8</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span></div><p>You pay $\\text{RM}20{,}000$ into a fund whose front-end load is $4\\%$ <strong>of the offering price</strong>. NAV is $\\text{RM}12.50$. How many shares do you receive?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> $1{,}600.00$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> $1{,}536.00$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> $1{,}538.46$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> $1{,}476.92$</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Amount invested $= 20{,}000 \\times (1 - 0.04) = \\text{RM}19{,}200$; shares $= \\dfrac{19{,}200}{12.50} = \\mathbf{1{,}536}$. Equivalently, offer price $= \\dfrac{12.50}{0.96} = \\text{RM}13.021$ and $\\dfrac{20{,}000}{13.021} = 1{,}536$. A ignores the load; C treats the load as $4\\%$ of NAV ($\\dfrac{20{,}000}{12.50 \\times 1.04}$), the wrong base; D divides the invested amount by $\\text{RM}13$, double counting the load.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D9</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span></div><p>A closed-end fund has NAV $\\text{RM}15.00$ and you buy at a $12\\%$ discount. A year later NAV is $\\text{RM}16.20$, the discount has narrowed to $5\\%$, and you received a $\\text{RM}0.40$ cash distribution. What is <strong>your</strong> holding-period return?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> $10.67\\%$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> $16.59\\%$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> $17.27\\%$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> $19.62\\%$</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Buy price $= 15 \\times 0.88 = \\text{RM}13.20$; sell price $= 16.20 \\times 0.95 = \\text{RM}15.39$. $HPR = \\dfrac{15.39 - 13.20 + 0.40}{13.20} = \\dfrac{2.59}{13.20} = \\mathbf{19.62\\%}$. A is the NAV return $\\dfrac{1.20 + 0.40}{15}$, which ignores the narrowing discount you benefited from; B leaves out the distribution; C divides by NAV instead of the price you paid.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D10</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span></div><p>Investors A and B each put $\\text{RM}10{,}000$ into the same fund, which earns $8\\%$ a year net of expenses for five years. A pays a $3\\%$ front-end load on the amount paid. B pays no front-end load but a $3\\%$ back-end load on the redemption value. Who ends with more?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Investor A, because paying the load early means it is over and done with, and the remaining balance then compounds free of any further charge.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Investor B, because deferring a cost always increases the ending value, since the full $\\text{RM}10{,}000$ earns $8\\%$ a year for all five years.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> They end with the same amount: both multiply the same sum by $0.97$ and by $1.08^{5}$, and the order of multiplication does not matter.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Investor A, because the back-end load is charged on a larger, grown balance, so B gives up more ringgit at redemption than A paid at the start.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>$A = 10{,}000(0.97)(1.08)^{5} = \\text{RM}14{,}252.48$ and $B = 10{,}000(1.08)^{5}(0.97) = \\text{RM}14{,}252.48$. D is the tempting trap: B's load is larger in ringgit, but A's load also removed money that would have grown. The equality breaks if the back-end load is charged on a different base (such as the lower of cost or value) or shrinks with the holding period, which is why the stated terms matter.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D11</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span></div><p>A fund reports a one-year return of $6.5\\%$ after operating expenses (expense ratio $0.9\\%$); its matched benchmark returned $7.0\\%$. An investor bought at the start of the year paying a $2\\%$ front-end load on the amount paid. Which statement is correct?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The fund trailed its benchmark by $1.4$ points, because the expense ratio must be subtracted from the reported return first.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The investor earned about $4.37\\%$, and the fund trailed its benchmark by $0.5$ points.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The investor earned $4.5\\%$, because the load is simply subtracted from the fund's return.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The fund beat its benchmark by $0.4$ points, because expenses should be added back before comparing.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Investor: $0.98 \\times 1.065 - 1 = \\mathbf{4.37\\%}$; the load reduces the amount that earns $6.5\\%$, so it is not a simple subtraction (C). Fund: $6.5\\% - 7.0\\% = -0.5$ points; the $0.9\\%$ is already inside the $6.5\\%$, so subtracting it again (A) double counts. Adding expenses back (D) measures the portfolio before costs, which investors cannot earn.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D12</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span></div><p>An investor already owns $30$ technology shares and adds a technology-sector ETF holding $120$ companies. What does this do to the portfolio's risk?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> It mainly increases concentration in one sector: the ETF spreads company-specific risk within technology but adds to a sector exposure the investor already has.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> It removes market risk, because the ETF holds four times as many companies.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> It has no effect on risk, because ETFs are passive.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> It diversifies the portfolio across sectors and styles, because the ETF owns $120$ companies, most of which the investor does not already hold, so the overlap is small.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>The number of holdings is not the number of risk sources. A sector shock hits the investor's own technology shares and the ETF together. Market risk cannot be diversified away (B), passive management says nothing about risk (C), and new names in the same sector do not add new sectors (D).</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D13</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span></div><p>A closed-end fund trades at a $15\\%$ discount to NAV, uses leverage and has an expense ratio of $1.8\\%$. An open-end fund in the same asset class charges $0.6\\%$. A colleague says: \"The closed-end fund is clearly better: every ringgit buys $\\text{RM}1.18$ of assets.\" Which evaluation is strongest?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Agree: a discount is risk-free extra value that must eventually be paid out, because the fund's assets will be distributed at NAV when it winds up, so the extra $\\text{RM}0.18$ per ringgit is locked in from the day of purchase.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Disagree: closed-end funds can never be bought below NAV, because arbitrageurs would buy the shares and redeem them, so the quoted discount must be an error.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Agree, because leverage always raises the long-run return of a fund, and together with the discount it more than covers the extra $1.2$ points a year of expenses.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Partly true but incomplete: $\\dfrac{1}{0.85} = 1.18$ ringgit of assets per ringgit is real, but the discount may persist or widen, the extra $1.2$ points a year of expenses erode returns, and leverage magnifies losses.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Buying at a discount does give more assets (and often more distributions) per ringgit invested, but that gain is only realized as a capital gain if the discount narrows, and ongoing higher costs and leverage risk count against it. A treats an observation as a guarantee; B is false, discounts are common; C ignores that leverage magnifies losses as well as gains.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D14</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span></div><p>A short-term government bond fund returned $4\\%$ in a year when a broad stock index returned $15\\%$ and a matched short-term government bond index returned $3.6\\%$. A board member says the manager \"underperformed by $11$ points.\" Which response is best?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Agree: a broad stock index is the standard benchmark for every fund, because it shows what investors could have earned that year by simply holding the whole stock market instead.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Agree: a manager should always beat the best-performing asset class.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Disagree: against a matched short-term government bond benchmark the fund is ahead by $0.4$ points; a broad stock index has different risks and is not a fair yardstick.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Disagree: bond funds cannot be compared with any benchmark.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Relative return must use a benchmark with the same kind of assets and risk: $4\\% - 3.6\\% = +0.4$ points. Comparing a bond fund with equities (A, B) mixes up asset-class choice with manager skill. Bond funds can and should be benchmarked (D).</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D15</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span></div><p>A saver will invest $\\text{RM}100$ a month. Option 1: a no-load index mutual fund, expense ratio $0.30\\%$. Option 2: an index ETF tracking the same index, expense ratio $0.10\\%$, with a $\\text{RM}7$ commission on every purchase. Which evaluation is most accurate?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The ETF is cheaper from the first month, because its expense ratio is one-third of the mutual fund's.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The two are equal, because both track the same index.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The commission does not matter because it is not part of the expense ratio.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The ETF is far more expensive for this saver at first: commissions cost $12 \\times 7 = \\text{RM}84$ a year, while the $0.2$-point expense saving on a first-year average balance of about $\\text{RM}600$ is only about $\\text{RM}1.20$. The saving matches the commissions only when the balance reaches about $\\dfrac{84}{0.002} = \\text{RM}42{,}000$.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>A $\\text{RM}7$ commission is $7\\%$ of each $\\text{RM}100$ purchase. The expense-ratio advantage grows with the balance, so it can win in the long run, especially if the saver buys less often. A ignores per-trade costs; B ignores all cost differences; C confuses \"not in the expense ratio\" with \"not a cost\".</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D16</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span></div><p>Which monitoring plan for an investor's fund holdings is best designed?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Review at fixed intervals and after triggers (manager or mandate change, fee rise, concentration shift, new goals), comparing after-cost returns with a matched benchmark.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Each year, sell and switch into whichever fund in the same category had the highest return over the previous twelve months, so the portfolio always holds the most recent winner.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Never review a fund once it has been chosen, because trading costs money.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Review only when the NAV falls by more than $10\\%$, and then sell immediately.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>A good process is periodic plus event-driven and uses evidence on both the fund (mandate, costs, holdings, benchmark-relative results) and the investor. B chases short-term performance, which is a weak predictor and adds costs; C ignores changes in the fund and the investor; D reacts only to price and may sell after a market-wide fall that says nothing about the fund's quality.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D17</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span></div><p>An investor has $\\text{RM}100{,}000$: $\\text{RM}20{,}000$ is needed for a house deposit in nine months, and the rest is for retirement in $25$ years. The investor already holds a large position in bank shares. Which plan is most defensible?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Put everything into a high-return equity sector fund and withdraw the deposit when needed.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Keep the $\\text{RM}20{,}000$ in short-term, liquid holdings; put the $\\text{RM}80{,}000$ in diversified, low-cost equity funds with little overlap with the bank shares; set review triggers.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Put all $\\text{RM}100{,}000$ in a money market fund until retirement because it is the safest category.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Split the money equally among ten equity funds from different fund families, because holding more funds always means more diversification, whatever each fund owns and whenever the money is needed.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Match each pot of money to its horizon and liquidity need, then diversify the long-term pot against the investor's existing concentration. A exposes the near-term need to a volatile, concentrated fund; C sacrifices decades of growth and is not risk-free either; D counts funds, not exposures, and ten funds can share the same holdings.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D18</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span></div><p>You must design one number to compare two funds with different loads, expense ratios and trading costs for a particular investor. Which design is best?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The projected ending value (or its annualized equivalent) after all loads, ongoing expenses and trading costs, over the investor's own holding period and contribution pattern, with the same gross return for both funds.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The expense ratio alone, since it is the only fee charged every year and so, over any realistic holding period, it outweighs loads, trading costs and other one-off charges combined.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The sales load alone, since it is the largest single charge an investor pays and is taken before any of the money starts earning a return.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The sum of the load percentage and the expense ratio, ignoring the holding period, since both are stated as percentages of the amount invested and so can be added directly into one total-cost figure for each fund.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Costs differ in timing (entry, annual, exit, per trade), so only a projection over the investor's own horizon and buying pattern puts them on one basis. B and C each ignore part of the cost; D adds a one-off percentage to an annual one, which overstates the load's effect for long horizons and understates the expense ratio's.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension questions</span><small>beyond the notes, lowest Bloom level first</small></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X2</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Industry</span></div><p>Open-end funds fill orders at the <strong>next</strong> NAV calculated after the order is received (\"forward pricing\"). Why does the industry insist on this?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> It guarantees that every order is filled at a higher price than the previous day's NAV, protecting the fund's remaining holders from investors who sell into a falling market.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> It stops investors from trading at a stale, already-known NAV after news has moved markets, which would transfer value from long-term shareholders to fast traders.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> It allows the fund to avoid calculating NAV on most days, since orders can be pooled and filled together at the next weekly valuation.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> It lets the investment adviser choose the price at which each investor trades, so large orders can be filled without moving the fund's published NAV for everyone else.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>If orders could be filled at an earlier NAV after prices had moved (for example after a foreign market closed), late traders could buy at yesterday's lower value and dilute existing holders. Forward pricing removes that option. It guarantees no price (A), still requires a daily NAV (C), and gives no discretion to the adviser (D).</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X1</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Industry</span></div><p>A fund operations team accrues the expense ratio daily. Net assets are $\\text{RM}500$ million and the expense ratio is $0.73\\%$ a year, accrued over $365$ days. What is the daily expense accrual (holding net assets constant)?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> $\\text{RM}3{,}650{,}000$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> $\\text{RM}1{,}000$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> $\\text{RM}10{,}000$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> $\\text{RM}100{,}000$</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>$\\dfrac{500{,}000{,}000 \\times 0.0073}{365} = \\mathbf{\\text{RM}10{,}000}$ a day. A is the whole year's expense; B and D are off by a factor of ten. Daily accrual is why expenses appear gradually as a slightly lower NAV rather than as a bill.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X3</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · CFA</span></div><p>An ETF trades at $\\text{RM}25.30$ while the value of its basket (indicative NAV) is $\\text{RM}25.00$. A creation unit is $50{,}000$ shares. Creating a unit costs a fixed fee of $\\text{RM}3{,}000$ plus trading costs of $0.4\\%$ of the basket's value. Ignoring price moves during the trade, what is the authorized participant's profit from creating one unit and selling it?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> $\\text{RM}15{,}000$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> $\\text{RM}12{,}000$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> $\\text{RM}10{,}000$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> $\\text{RM}7{,}000$</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Gross gap $= 50{,}000 \\times (25.30 - 25.00) = \\text{RM}15{,}000$. Basket value $= 50{,}000 \\times 25 = \\text{RM}1{,}250{,}000$, so trading costs $= 0.004 \\times 1{,}250{,}000 = \\text{RM}5{,}000$. Profit $= 15{,}000 - 3{,}000 - 5{,}000 = \\mathbf{\\text{RM}7{,}000}$. A ignores costs; B subtracts only the fee; C only the trading costs. If costs exceeded the gap, the premium could persist: arbitrage is bounded by costs.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X4</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · CFA</span></div><p>An index fund with an expense ratio of $0.20\\%$ returned $9.70\\%$ in a year when its index returned $10.00\\%$. Which interpretation is most accurate?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The $-0.30$-point tracking difference is larger than the expense ratio; the extra $0.10$ point likely comes from transaction costs, cash drag or sampling.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The fund's tracking error is $0.30\\%$, which measures how volatile its deviations are.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The fund must be actively managed, because a true index fund matches its index exactly.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The fund underperformed by $0.50$ points, because the $0.20\\%$ expense ratio has to be subtracted from the reported $9.70\\%$ before it is compared with the index's $10.00\\%$.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Tracking difference is the return gap ($9.70\\% - 10.00\\% = -0.30$ points). Expenses explain $0.20$; the rest comes from frictions such as trading costs, uninvested cash and sampling. Tracking error (B) is the standard deviation of such gaps over time, not a single gap. Index funds never track perfectly (C), and the reported return is already after expenses (D).</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X5</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Case competition</span></div><p>A client holds $60\\%$ of her portfolio in Fund P, which has $45\\%$ of its assets in technology, and $40\\%$ in Fund Q, a broad-market ETF with $28\\%$ in technology. What is her overall technology weight?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> $36.5\\%$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> $28\\%$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> $38.2\\%$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> $73\\%$</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Look through both funds and weight by the client's allocation: $0.60 \\times 45\\% + 0.40 \\times 28\\% = 27\\% + 11.2\\% = \\mathbf{38.2\\%}$. A is the simple average of the two funds' weights, ignoring the client's split; B looks only at the broad fund; D adds the two percentages. A \"diversified\" second fund can still leave a large sector concentration.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X7</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Master's</span></div><p>A taxable investor buys a fund at NAV $\\text{RM}15.00$ the day before a $\\text{RM}1.20$ capital-gain distribution. NAV falls to $\\text{RM}13.80$ when the distribution is paid in cash, and the investor pays tax of $20\\%$ on it. With no other price movement, what is the investor's immediate after-tax return?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> $0\\%$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> $+8.0\\%$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> $-8.0\\%$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> $-1.6\\%$</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>After the distribution the investor holds a share worth $\\text{RM}13.80$ plus $\\text{RM}1.20$ cash, less tax of $0.20 \\times 1.20 = \\text{RM}0.24$: $\\dfrac{13.80 + 1.20 - 0.24 - 15}{15} = \\mathbf{-1.6\\%}$. Before tax the return is $0\\%$ (A), because a distribution only moves value out of NAV; the tax makes \"buying the distribution\" costly. B counts the distribution as new wealth; C counts only the NAV drop.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X9</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · PhD</span></div><p>Why can closed-end fund discounts persist for years when ETF premiums and discounts are usually small?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Closed-end funds may report NAV only once a year, so traders cannot see the discount until the annual report arrives, by which time it has already changed.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Closed-end fund investors are legally barred from short selling, so arbitrageurs cannot take the other side when the shares trade away from NAV, and gaps are left to close on their own.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> No creation or redemption channel exists; an arbitrageur who buys the fund and shorts its holdings bears costs and the risk that the discount widens, with no forced convergence date.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Closed-end funds hold only illiquid assets, so NAV cannot be calculated reliably and the share price has no fixed, published value toward which it could converge, even over many years.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Limits to arbitrage explain persistence: the trade is costly, may require holding a widening position for a long time (noise-trader risk), and convergence is forced only if the fund is open-ended or liquidated. Sentiment-based explanations of the closed-end fund puzzle rest on exactly this risk. A, B and D are false: NAVs are reported frequently (often daily or weekly), shorting is generally possible, and many closed-end funds hold liquid listed securities.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X6</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Case competition</span></div><p>A new closed-end fund is offered to the public at $\\text{RM}10.00$ a share, but $5\\%$ of the proceeds goes to underwriting and offering costs. A client wants to buy at the offering. Which assessment is best?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Buy: an initial offering price always equals NAV, so there is no premium to worry about.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Caution: after costs the fund starts with NAV of about $\\text{RM}9.50$, so the client pays a premium of $\\dfrac{10 - 9.50}{9.50} \\approx 5.26\\%$; if the price later moves toward or below NAV, as it has for many new closed-end funds, buying later in the secondary market may be cheaper.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Buy: the offering costs are paid by the fund sponsor, not by investors.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Avoid all closed-end funds, because they always trade at a premium.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Offering costs come out of the money raised, so NAV per share starts below the price paid. That built-in premium disappears if the shares settle near or below NAV. A and C misstate where the costs fall; D is wrong because closed-end funds frequently trade at discounts, not always premiums.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X8</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Master's</span></div><p>A report says: \"The average 10-year return of the funds that exist in this category today is $9\\%$ a year, versus $8.5\\%$ for the index, so active management wins.\" Which evaluation is strongest?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The comparison is biased upward: funds that closed or merged after poor results are missing (survivorship bias), and risk differences and investors' actual costs are ignored; it does not show that active management wins.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The conclusion holds: an average taken over ten years and across every fund in the category removes luck, so the half-point advantage over the index must reflect the managers' genuine stock-picking skill.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The conclusion holds, because the index includes no costs at all, so beating it after fees is an even stronger sign of skill.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The comparison understates active returns, because the surviving funds are the worst performers, while many of the best managers left to run private funds whose results are excluded from the average.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Poor performers are more likely to be closed or merged away, so averaging today's survivors overstates the category's historical return. Ten years still contains noise (B). The point that indexes are cost-free (C) argues for caution about the index, not for accepting a biased fund average. D reverses the direction of survivorship bias.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X10</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · PhD</span></div><p>Studies find little persistence in mutual fund outperformance. A student concludes: \"So no fund manager has skill.\" In the Berk and Green (2004) model, why is this conclusion not justified?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Because in that model past winners always continue to outperform after fees.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Because investors move money toward managers with demonstrated skill; with decreasing returns to scale and fees set by the manager, expected alpha to investors is driven to about zero, so skill appears in fund size and fees rather than in persistent net outperformance.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Because the model assumes fund managers have no skill at all.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Because the model shows that index funds always underperform active funds.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>In Berk and Green, flows chase performance until the extra capital erodes the manager's edge, so investors earn about the benchmark net of fees even when the manager is skilled. Non-persistence is therefore consistent with skill. A contradicts the evidence the model explains; C reverses its key assumption; D is not a result of the model.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X11</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Postdoc</span></div><p>During a period of severe stress in corporate bond markets, several large bond ETFs traded at discounts of around $5\\%$ to their published NAVs. Which interpretation is most defensible?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The ETFs malfunctioned; their market prices were simply wrong and the NAVs were accurate, because NAVs are built from the bonds' official evaluated values, which are set by independent pricing services rather than by panicked sellers on the exchange.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The discounts prove that ETF creation and redemption never works, since arbitrageurs should have bought the ETF shares and redeemed them for bonds within the day.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Part of the \"discount\" reflected stale NAVs based on evaluated prices for bonds that were barely trading, so the ETF price may have been the timelier signal, while dealers' limited balance sheets also constrained the arbitrage.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The discounts show that bond ETFs should be priced only once a day like mutual funds, so that investors always trade at NAV and stress cannot open a gap.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>When the underlying bonds do not trade, NAV relies on evaluated or stale prices, while the ETF keeps trading and incorporates new information; and arbitrage needs dealers able to absorb bonds. Both effects widen measured discounts. A assumes the NAV was right; B overgeneralizes from stress conditions; D throws away the price discovery the exchange provided.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X12</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Postdoc</span></div><p>You want to test whether an ETF's measured premiums and discounts reflect genuine mispricing or merely stale NAVs. Which research design is most informative?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Count how many days the premium is positive and conclude mispricing if it is more than half.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Compare the ETF's expense ratio with that of an index mutual fund.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Survey retail investors about whether they believe the ETF is fairly priced.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Regress the next period's NAV return and the next period's ETF price return on today's premium: if the premium predicts the NAV catching up, it reflects staleness; if it predicts the ETF price reverting, it reflects mispricing. Control for the liquidity of the underlying assets and the timing of the NAV strike.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>The two explanations make opposite predictions about which price moves next, so a predictive regression separates them, and controls for underlying liquidity and timestamps address the main confounders. A counts signs without testing either mechanism; B measures costs, not pricing; C measures beliefs, not prices.</p></div></details></div></details></div></div></details>");
